From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Sun, 06 Sep 2026 01:08:01 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] xen/arm: let a device model share its own memory into its guest

A device model sharing its own memory into the guest it serves is refused on
Arm twice over, and both refusals have to go.

First, since Xen 4.22 the XENMAPSPACE_gmfn_foreign owner lookup on Arm is
rcu_lock_remote_domain_by_id(), which by construction cannot name the caller:
DOMID_SELF (0x7FF0) matches no domain's id, so it returns -ESRCH, and the
caller's real domid hits its own "*d == current->domain" test and returns
-EPERM. Until 4.22 this code called get_pg_owner(), which resolves DOMID_SELF
to current->domain -- that is how a device model names its own pages. (x86 is
not a precedent either way: its own p2m_add_foreign() uses
rcu_lock_remote_domain_by_id() and rejects tdom == fdom, and get_pg_owner()
survives there only for the MMU-update hypercalls.) Resolve DOMID_SELF here so
the caller can be the owner
again, and move the pre-existing "od == d" refusal out of the remote branch so
that it still covers the new one: a domain must not map its own page into its
own physmap this way.

Second, the remaining check is xsm_map_gmfn_foreign(XSM_TARGET, d, od), which
under the dummy policy asks whether the domain *receiving* the mapping (d) is
privileged over the page owner (od). That is the right question for the usual
direction -- a control domain mapping a guest's page into its own physmap --
and it denies the mirror image of it: here d is the guest and od is the driver
domain, so it fails with -EPERM.

The mirror direction is what a virtio-gpu host-visible blob needs on Arm.
Without it the blob window stays device-model MMIO and every guest access to it
traps out to the device model for emulation, which on arm64 is not merely slow
but fatal: a data abort from a SIMD/FP access carries no valid syndrome, so Xen
has to decode the instruction itself, and decode_arm64() covers only the
general-purpose load/store forms. Mesa's venus writes its command ring with
memcpy(), which is SIMD on aarch64, so the guest's Vulkan client dies at its
first ring write with

  (XEN) dNvM unhandled Arm instruction 0x3d800000
  (XEN) dNvM Unable to decode instruction

measured on a Raspberry Pi 5 with the qemu device model in a driver domain, and
the matching device-model side logging

  xen-arm: 33 of 33 pages of device RAM at 0x3a000000 were not shared into dom25
           (first error -1)

So allow the case where the caller owns the pages it is sharing. The caller is
already required to be privileged over the target domain by
xsm_add_to_physmap(XSM_TARGET, current->domain, d) in the common code above, so
the effective rule becomes "a domain privileged over the target may share its
own memory into that target". This grants the guest nothing: the owner is
resolved from the caller rather than named by the guest, and naming the target
itself is refused. The xsm_map_gmfn_foreign() check is left in place for every
other owner, so a Flask policy still governs third-party sharing.

A Flask rule was considered first and does not reach this case. The check that
fires is xsm_map_gmfn_foreign(XSM_TARGET, d, od), whose Flask implementation
asks for mmu:map_read/map_write on the OWNER's object in the RECEIVER's
context -- so granting it means granting the guest that permission over the
driver domain, which is strictly worse than what this patch does (the guest
gains nothing; only a caller sharing its OWN pages is newly allowed). And it
would not be reached in this product's default configuration anyway: the build
has CONFIG_XSM_FLASK=y but CONFIG_XSM_SILO_DEFAULT=y and no policy blob, and
xsm/silo.c overrides only the evtchn and grant hooks -- so this hook falls
through to the dummy implementation described above. Hence the structural fix
here and the upstream note below.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [needs discussion: upstream would likely want a
 new XSM hook, e.g. xsm_share_own_gmfn(), and the same relaxation on x86]
---
 xen/arch/arm/mm.c | 45 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++---------
 1 file changed, 36 insertions(+), 9 deletions(-)

--- a/xen/arch/arm/mm.c
+++ b/xen/arch/arm/mm.c
@@ -193,24 +193,51 @@ int xenmem_add_to_physmap_one(
 
         if ( extra.foreign_domid == DOMID_XEN )
             od = rcu_lock_domain(dom_xen);
+        else if ( extra.foreign_domid == DOMID_SELF )
+            /*
+             * rcu_lock_remote_domain_by_id() refuses DOMID_SELF (no domain has
+             * that id, so it is -ESRCH) and refuses the caller's own id
+             * (-EPERM), so resolve DOMID_SELF here. This is what get_pg_owner()
+             * did -- the lookup this code used until Xen 4.22 -- and it is how
+             * a device model names its own pages.
+             */
+            od = rcu_lock_current_domain();
         else
         {
             rc = rcu_lock_remote_domain_by_id(extra.foreign_domid, &od);
             if ( rc )
                 return rc;
-
-            if ( od == d )
-            {
-                rcu_unlock_domain(od);
-                return -EINVAL;
-            }
         }
 
-        rc = xsm_map_gmfn_foreign(XSM_TARGET, d, od);
-        if ( rc )
+        /*
+         * Moved out of the branch above so that it also covers DOMID_SELF: a
+         * domain must not map its own page into its own physmap this way.
+         */
+        if ( od == d )
         {
             rcu_unlock_domain(od);
-            return rc;
+            return -EINVAL;
+        }
+
+        /*
+         * xsm_map_gmfn_foreign() asks whether the domain receiving the mapping
+         * is privileged over the page owner, which is the right question when a
+         * control domain maps a guest's page into its own physmap. A device
+         * model sharing its own memory into the guest it serves is the opposite
+         * direction, so ask the question that fits it instead: the caller owns
+         * the pages, and the common code has already required the caller to be
+         * privileged over the target domain (xsm_add_to_physmap()). The guest
+         * cannot initiate this -- the owner is resolved from the caller, not
+         * named by the guest, and the od == d case is refused above.
+         */
+        if ( od != current->domain )
+        {
+            rc = xsm_map_gmfn_foreign(XSM_TARGET, d, od);
+            if ( rc )
+            {
+                rcu_unlock_domain(od);
+                return rc;
+            }
         }
 
         /* Take reference to the foreign domain page.
